# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the tests of a sanitized build
# (ALAddTestBinary.cmake gives the runs this file), and for runs of its
# viewer. One per line:
#
#   leak:<function, source file or library>
#
# A suppression is for code that is not ours -- a system library, a driver
# -- and says above it what it hides, why it cannot be fixed here, and where
# it was reported upstream. A leak in our own code is fixed, not listed, and
# one in a port the Alchemy registry carries is patched there.
#
# The gl tests keep every library loaded until the process ends
# (llheadlessgl_fixture.h): an unloaded driver's globals hold nothing, and
# all it keeps for the process would read as leaked. Mesa's llvmpipe, which
# the tests draw with, leaks nothing then.

# NVIDIA's EGL driver, on a host that has it: libglvnd loads every vendor's
# EGL library it finds, so it is loaded beside Mesa even where the tests draw
# on llvmpipe, and its initialisation (NvGlEglApiInit) keeps blocks nothing
# holds once it is done. Closed source and the system's; not on CI's runners.
leak:libnvidia-eglcore

# fontconfig, vcpkg's port: a pattern holds its array of elements as an
# offset from itself (elts_offset, fcint.h), not a pointer, so LeakSanitizer,
# which follows pointers, finds the array of every pattern fontconfig keeps
# for the process unreachable. A config that selects fonts by pattern keeps
# one from the first FcInit -- Ubuntu's 70-no-bitmaps.conf rejects bitmap
# fonts with <rejectfont><pattern>, so CI's runners do, where Fedora's
# config has none -- and every process that asked fontconfig for a fallback
# font reported 288 to 320 bytes. Only the array's allocation is hidden: a
# pattern that does leak is still reported by its own (FcPatternCreate). Not
# a fontconfig bug to report: the offsets are its cache's format.
leak:FcPatternObjectInsertElt

# The same offsets in the system's fontconfig, which GTK loads for libdecor's
# GTK plugin: SDL has libdecor draw a Wayland window's frame where the
# compositor leaves that to the client, as GNOME's does. Pango caches the font
# sets GTK measures the title with, for the process, and their patterns'
# arrays read as leaked, but the system's library is stripped, so
# FcPatternObjectInsertElt has no name there to match. Matched instead through
# Pango's fontconfig backend, which nothing of ours calls.
leak:libpangoft2-1.0.so

# libdecor's GTK plugin, the system's, leaves a Wayland proxy it made while
# committing a frame's configuration (wl_proxy_marshal_flags, from
# libdecor_frame_commit) when SDL destroys the frame: 96 bytes, now and then,
# as the window goes. Not ours, and the plugin is stripped.
leak:libdecor-gtk.so
